   "source": "---\n\n## PM Commentary — What I'd Build Next\n\nThis notebook is a fast, transparent scoring model. The data model and weights are the intellectual work; the Python is just expression of it. Here's how it would evolve as a real Commerce product capability:\n\n### 1 — Connect to live merchant data\nThe current model uses manually-specified merchant profiles. The natural next step is pulling from BigCommerce's Merchants API: real AOV (from Orders), real traffic split (from Analytics), real geo distribution (from Storefront). The scoring then runs automatically per merchant, not per archetype.\n\n### 2 — Benchmark against cohort peers\nFor each merchant, compare their payment method mix against cohort peers (same MCC, similar AOV band, same geography). Surface the gap: \"B2B merchants in your revenue band with ACH enabled see 8% lower cart abandonment.\"\n\n### 3 — Wire to Commerce Companion\nThe Commerce Live 2026 roadmap introduced Commerce Companion — an AI assistant embedded in the BC admin. A natural integration: surface the scorer's output as a Companion recommendation (\"3 payment methods could improve your conversion — here's why\"). Uses the same reasoning model; just changes the surface.\n\n### 4 — A/B hypothesis generator\nGiven the ranked list, generate pre-formatted A/B test hypotheses for the top 2–3 recommendations. Include: hypothesis statement, primary metric, minimum detectable effect, estimated sample size at current traffic. This moves from \"what to add\" to \"how to validate it.\"\n\n### 5 — Agentic checkout readiness score\nSeparate from the per-method scoring: assess the merchant's overall checkout stack readiness for agent-driven purchases (Commerce's explicitly stated 2026 direction). Factors: Checkout SDK integration, stored instrument support, Apple Pay / Google Pay presence, headless storefront capability. Output a readiness score with a specific gap list.\n\n---\n\n*Built using publicly available BigCommerce developer documentation, gateway pages, and Commerce Live 2026 product announcements. All conversion lift estimates are drawn from published payment provider research; actual uplift varies by merchant context.*"
